## Goal
Create a principal component analysis (PCA) of the six RNA-seq samples from the gene counts and produce one sample plot coloured by treated versus untreated condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |

## Method
1. Run a Python script in the `gars-bio` conda environment via the configured batch executor. Resolve the `counts_gene` input at execution time using `_system/resolve_artifact.py`; read the table with gene identifiers and sample columns, and verify that the six sample columns match `A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3`.
2. Use the gene-level counts as provided. Exclude genes with zero total count across all samples, apply `log2(count + 1)`, center each gene across samples, and run PCA on the centered matrix with scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)`. Do not scale genes to unit variance. These transformations are in memory only and do not modify the input.
3. Create one scatter plot of PC1 versus PC2, with one point per sample, labels `A1` through `B3`, and colours keyed to the project's design: `untreated` for A1-A3 and `treated` for B1-B3. Label axes with the percent variance explained by each component and include a legend.
4. Save the PCA coordinates and variance fractions as a CSV, and save the plot as a PNG under `results/`.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| results/sample_pca_coordinates.csv | table | PC1 and PC2 coordinates, sample IDs, condition, and component variance fractions |
| results/sample_pca.png | figure | PC1 versus PC2 sample scatter plot, coloured by treated versus untreated |

## Execution
Runs: batch
Expected wall time: under 10 minutes. Expected output size: under 10 MB.
